=== SIGNATURE ===
scan2(start_a, start_b, arr, lambda_a, lambda_b)

=== COMMENT ===
Like SCAN but carries TWO accumulators. For each item of arr it updates a and b together; lambda_a and lambda_b each receive (a, b, item) and return the next a and next b. Returns a 2-column array of the running (a, b) after each item.

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(start_a, start_b, arr, lambda_a, lambda_b, LET(
   vec, TOCOL(arr),
   seeded, REDUCE(HSTACK(start_a, start_b), vec, LAMBDA(acc, item, LET(
      cur_a, INDEX(acc, ROWS(acc), 1),
      cur_b, INDEX(acc, ROWS(acc), 2),
      VSTACK(acc, HSTACK(lambda_a(cur_a, cur_b, item), lambda_b(cur_a, cur_b, item)))
   ))),
   DROP(seeded, 1)
))

=== DESCRIPTION ===
A two-accumulator SCAN. Ordinary SCAN can only carry one scalar accumulator, so carrying two values forces you to concatenate them into one; scan2 threads two independent accumulators for you.

Arguments:
  start_a  - initial value of the first accumulator.
  start_b  - initial value of the second accumulator.
  arr      - the items to iterate over (flattened top-to-bottom, like TOCOL).
  lambda_a - LAMBDA(a, b, item) returning the next value of accumulator a. It sees the CURRENT a and b and the current item.
  lambda_b - LAMBDA(a, b, item) returning the next value of accumulator b (also sees the current a and b).

Returns:
  A 2-column array with one row per item of arr: column 1 is the running a, column 2 is the running b, each taken AFTER that item is processed (matching SCAN's per-item semantics). Take the last row for the final pair.

Both new values are computed from the PRE-update a and b, so lambda_a and lambda_b can reference each other's previous value. Accumulators should be scalars (numbers or text), like SCAN.

Example - running sum in a, running max in b, over {3;1;4;1;5}:
  scan2(0, -1E+308, {3;1;4;1;5}, LAMBDA(a,b,x, a + x), LAMBDA(a,b,x, MAX(b, x)))
  -> {3,3; 4,3; 8,4; 9,4; 14,5}

Note: builds the result by appending rows (order n^2), so it suits small/medium arrays. See scan3 for three accumulators.
